Galaxy 1: Well here we are again.

Galaxy 2: It seems the Lomans have come to the climax of their story. But how will it end?

Galaxy 1: What do you believe the garden represents for Willy? Suddenly planting... he himself said nothing would grow.

Galaxy 2: I believe the garden represents Willy's one last hope of legacy.
Galaxy 2: For so long he desired to raise these successful sons with his faulty advice. So badly he wanted his sons to 'traverse the jungle'.
Galaxy 2: Now that the realization of his sons' failures has set in, he lashes and dissociates in one last effort to cling to the future he and his family could have had.
Galaxy 2: But in his actions he shows the truth. He knows that he has failed, whether he would admit it or not, and so he plants this garden to give his final, and only, legacy.

Galaxy 1: Interesting...
Galaxy 1: Furthermore, the presence of Ben shows that Willy never gave up on his morals.
Galaxy 1: He talks with Ben of a proposition for thousands of dollars, of which comes from his life insurance payout after killing himself.
Galaxy 1: He has not apologized or forgiven anyone. He is still in denial of the impacts of his actions. He is only desperate to pull his family out of this situation which, whether he knows it or not, he put them in.
Galaxy 1: As grave as it is, his suicide to him is the only solution.
Galaxy 1: Even as he debates this final solution of his, Ben still talks of the jungle. Willy never let his hope for the American Dream go.

Galaxy 2: Let us continue now...

